import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Actors",
	description: "Actors on the Roc Solid server platform: state that handles one message at a time, in order, with typed methods, asks, tells and timers, kept in the database across restarts.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "kinds", label: "Kinds and methods" },
		{ id: "asking", label: "Asking and telling" },
		{ id: "timers", label: "Timers" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · actors"
				title="State, one message at a time"
			>
				A shopping cart, a counter, a game room: state that many requests change at once, and that must
				stay right. An <strong>actor</strong> holds it, handles one message at a time, in order, and keeps
				it in the database across restarts. For what only matters now (who is online, what was just said),
				use <a href={Paths.platform_live}>live state</a> in memory.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="kinds">Kinds and methods</Heading>
			<p>
				An actor is a <strong>kind</strong> and a <strong>key</strong>: the cart of member 7, the counter
				called <code>visits</code>. A kind has a name and a starting state (<code>initial</code>, computed
				from the key). Its <strong>methods</strong> are what its actors can do, each with its own input and
				reply types. A method's turn gets the actor (<code>me</code>), the app's context (what
				<code>init!</code> made), the current state and the input, and returns the next state and the
				reply.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_counter.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_counter.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Nobody creates or ends an actor.</strong> A call to any key finds that actor as its last
					turn left it, or new, starting from <code>initial(key)</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One turn at a time, in order.</strong> Each actor has a mailbox in the server and handles
					its messages one turn after another, whatever method each is for. A hundred asks at once each see
					the state the one before left: no lost updates, no locks to write.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A bad call is an error, not a crash.</strong> Methods are typed, so this is rare: a caller
					built against an older or newer kind may send an input a method doesn't take, or name a missing
					method. That, a crash inside a method, and an unknown kind are each logged as an error and
					reported to the caller. The actor's state stays as it was, and it goes on to its next turn.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Kept in the database</strong> (<code>rocx_actors</code>): read at an actor's first turn,
					written after each turn that changes it. An actor idle for 15 minutes
					(<code>--actor-idle-ms</code>) leaves memory; the next call wakes it from the database.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<strong>How the types fit together.</strong> A kind is a type of your app's with <code>name</code>
				and <code>initial</code> (checked through <code>Actor.Kind</code>). It names its methods' type once,
				<code>{"Method(input, reply) : Actor.Method(Counter, Str, Str, { count : I64 }, input, reply)"}</code>:
				the kind, the app's context, the key and the state. Each method is then a value,
				<code>{"Actor.method(name, run!)"}</code>, annotated with that type
				(<code>{"Counter.Method(I64, I64)"}</code>), which makes it that kind's.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Keys.</strong> A key is any type with <code>to_str</code>, which names its actor (in the
				database too), and <code>from_quote</code>, which reads that name back and lets a string literal be
				a key (<code>Actor.Keyed</code>). <code>Str</code> has both; so can your own type, like
				<code>{"[YouTube, Instagram(Str)]"}</code>. A method gets the key as its own type
				(<code>me.key()</code>), so it can match on it rather than parse it, and another kind's key type
				doesn't compile.
			</p>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> finds the methods your modules define (<code>Actor.method</code>, at the top
				of a module's block) and lists them in the program, so the server can run an actor when nobody
				waits on it: for a tell or a timer. It also checks that each kind's name
				(<code>{"name = |{}| \"…\""}</code>) is unique. Two methods of one kind with the same name stop the
				server at start.
			</p>

			<Heading id="asking">Asking and telling</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_ask.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_ask.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Counter.add.ask!(key, input)"}</code> waits for the reply, typed as the method says, or
				<code>ActorErr(why)</code> if none comes. <code>{"Counter.add.tell!(key, input)"}</code> doesn't
				wait: its turn comes after those before it, and its reply goes nowhere (a problem is logged).
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_tell.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_tell.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				An actor may ask another. An ask that would wait forever is refused at once: an actor asking
				itself, or any actor whose turn waits on its answer. An ask unanswered after 30 seconds is given
				up, and its message dropped if its turn hasn't come.
			</p>

			<Heading id="timers">Timers</Heading>
			<p>
				A method gets the actor itself, <code>me</code>: its key (<code>me.key()</code>) and its timers.
				<code>{"me.after!(ms, method, input)"}</code> calls one of its kind's methods later (another kind's
				doesn't compile) and returns the timer, which <code>Actor.cancel!</code> stops. To repeat, let each
				call set the next. A method that names itself is a function of <code>{"{}"}</code>, because a value
				can't be defined in terms of itself:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_ticker.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_ticker.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Counter.add.tell_after!(key, ms, input)"}</code> does the same from anywhere: a request, a
				job, another actor.
			</p>
			<p>
				With a database, a timer survives a restart. It is a row in <code>rocx_actor_timers</code>, written
				at once (outside the caller's open transaction, as a tell is sent at once) and deleted once its turn
				has ended; the server sets every row again at start. So a timer's call is made at least once: if the
				server is killed mid-turn, it runs again after the restart. <code>Actor.cancel!</code> stops a
				timer that is waiting, or one that fired but whose turn hasn't come. Without a database, timers are
				in memory, and a restart loses them.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>An actor's state is JSON in one row</strong>, written after each message that changes it:
					fine for a cart, not for a ledger.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Tells are in memory.</strong> A restart loses unhandled ones (timers survive); use a
					<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>job</a> for work that must survive one.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Every kind of state, side by side: <a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#which"}>which to use</a>.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The platform's tests ask an actor a hundred times at once, crash one, make asks that would wait
					forever, tell in order, set and cancel timers, send a message no method takes, idle one out and
					wake it, and restart the server: the state is kept, and the timer fires once.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
